plan OperationsV2
title   Operations and store semantics
status  archived
goal
    Realize the migrated operation semantics with one canonical resource store,
    actor-owned occurrences, generation fencing, and passive exact-key projections.
    This Plan owns migrated semantics under @plan.Verification.D1; source contracts
    supply provenance and trace IDs. Runtime identity belongs to @plan.RuntimeV2.O2 and
    @plan.RuntimeV2.O1. Actor lifecycle belongs to @plan.RuntimeV2.O5. Implementation and
    product cutover remain pending under @plan.Cutover.O3 and @plan.Cutover.V7.
outcomes
    O1  Canonical store and named resource operations
        One runtime-scoped StoreKernel owns one atomic StoreState. That state contains
        descriptor IDs, canonical keys, committed bases/metadata, lookup generations,
        ordered optimistic overlays, monotonic revision, and canonical-deduplicated
        changed refs. Every mutation/publication passes through this owner. Its concrete
        Effect primitive is private. Equal descriptor/K shares canonical data and
        generations only within one Runtime. Actors retain independent bindings,
        occurrences, projections, previews, and lifetimes.
        StoreKernel returns projections/revisions and never invokes actors.
        No competing canonical store, actor-private canonical cache, or overlay as shared
        truth. Releasing one actor cannot change another actor's lifetime/effective
        overlay. Cross-runtime browser roots, SSR requests, tests, and runtimes are
        isolated. Canonical identity is defined once by
        @plan.RuntimeV2.O2; descriptor/P/K/declaration vocabulary is owned by
        @plan.RuntimeV2.O1 and @plan.OperationsV2.O1.

        A canonical revision advances for base, generation, freshness, invalidation,
        hydration, and eviction changes. Applying/removing/replaying an actor overlay
        advances only that actor's publication/overlay cursor. StoreFanout is the only
        actor-facing StoreState path and owns the live mailbox registry plus exact
        dependency reverse index. It offers compact monotonic revision facts only to
        affected actors, after initiating snapshot publication, TurnRecord acceptance,
        and acknowledgment. Recipients ignore stale revisions/undeclared refs and reread
        current StoreState; adjacent projection-only facts may coalesce to newest revision
        and union of refs. Never coalesce lifecycle, terminal-operation, cancellation,
        stream-terminal, or mapped-event facts. Never subscribe actors directly to
        StoreState, run transitions/mutate snapshots in fanout, or expose stale historical
        payload as current. Owner-overlay facts go only to the initiating actor. Autonomous
        commits may lack an initiating actor barrier. Cross-actor reads remain tear-free
        and causally ordered; canonical changes reach affected actors after the initiating
        publication.

        DehydrateBarrier is package-private and shared by StoreKernel commit windows and
        capture. Capture observes one StoreState revision and stable actor leases under
        @plan.Artifacts.O3; unrelated state-only turns are ignored. Capture is allowed only
        after a completed commit/publication boundary, never between actor store commit
        and actor publication. The barrier is not a public consistency/transaction API.
        This Plan owns StoreState commit and barrier exclusion; actor publication/acknowledgment
        belongs to @plan.RuntimeV2.O4, and Story/checkpoint capture to
        @plan.RuntimeV2.O6.

        Generations fence stale completions even when interruption is requested. Canonical
        store revision increments for canonical facts; actor preview apply/remove/replay
        affects only initiating publication/overlay. Prepared actor/store revisions start
        at 0. First generation/value revision/global evidence sequence is 1. Controlled
        endpoint/subscription ordinals alone start at 0. Counters are non-negative safe
        integers, increment before mutation, and fail before wrap/reuse/partial publication.
        A collected store lifetime may restart generation/value revision at 1; actor and
        global counters never reset. Late authoritative facts cannot overwrite or
        republish current facts; async completion is a later actor turn. Public snapshot
        revision and other shared identity laws are owned by
        @plan.RuntimeV2.O2 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O5.


        Flat named resource, transaction, and stream descriptors expose actor-bound O.
        Keep descriptor access, reads, and work ownership separate:

        - Listing descriptors, calling `O.name.key(P)`, reading `getData(K)` or
        `getState(K)`, and constructing an unreturned plan are passive.
        - Resource lookup and refetch are finite. Subscribe is continuing ownership.
        `setData` is authoritative; `cancel(K)` is actor-owned finite cancellation.
        - Streams are actor-owned continuing operations, not StoreKernel entries.
        - An omitted transaction key projector means `K = []`.
        - Offline state never blocks explicit lookup/subscribe/refetch or cancels work.
        Reconnect/focus may refresh only active stale/missing subscriptions without an
        in-flight generation.

        Do not add a generic registry, implicit work, stream `cancel(K)`, browser-offline
        admission gate, or generic paused-resource policy. Applications may gate their
        own activities.

        Only accepted event actions admit finite lookup/refetch occurrences. `cancel(K)`
        cancels each current finite occurrence that the actor owns and whose key matches
        K. Record accepted interruption and suppress later results. Interrupt shared work
        only at its final owner. Cancellation never changes canonical data.

        Fresh canonical lookup settlement may avoid adapter work. Stale, missing, or
        invalidated lookup follows generation policy. Planned cancel, release, or
        supersession is evidence-only cleanup; it invokes no authored mapper. Each
        occurrence settles once. A canonical lookup success installs canonical data.

        Transaction or stream output is not canonical unless an accepted mapping calls
        `setData(K, value)`. Such a write fences older lookup generations and clears
        invalidation/failure metadata. It advances revision/generation, publishes once,
        and leaves continuing subscribers attached. An undefined updater declines.

        Arbitrary cache mutation, implicit response mapping, generic writer registries, and
        ordinary cache-clear escape hatches are forbidden. Boot, SSR, and fixture writes
        use package-private HostWriteLease only. Do not add a generic runtime registry or
        cache-clear escape hatch.

        Continuing resource subscription maps exactly value(A), failure(E), defect(),
        interrupt(). Activation publishes an existing canonical value once; later
        canonical replacements map through value. Identity is compiled declaration slot
        plus operation kind, exact descriptor, and K; neither plan object nor P is
        identity. Equal normalized plans retain work; changed slot/kind/descriptor/K
        releases the old binding once and admits the replacement. One shared generation
        pins one P. Equal-value/overlay-only changes do not emit value; planned release
        does not route interruption. Callers do not manage subscriptions; runtime-sized
        subscribe-many is excluded. StoreKernel owns exact-key shared lookup generation
        and temporary in-flight lease; actor scopes own registrations/activity leases.

        Invalidation and clearing are scoped actor actions returned by accepted event
        actions. Accept only exact [O.resource, K], declared reachable tags, and admitted
        families. Validate all targets; expand against one pre-mutation index in
        descriptor/K insertion order; first-seen deduplicate; reject invalid,
        unauthorized, or beyond-256 expansion before allocation/mutation. Missing exact
        targets and zero-match tags/families are successful no-ops. Nonempty invalidate
        retains data/overlays while marking stale. Nonempty clear fences/removes data,
        metadata, overlays, and interrupts under final-owner policy. Reject whole-runtime,
        wildcard, zero-argument clear, partial mixed batches, direct lookup start from
        invalidate, or conflicts that see a partially changed target set. Each nonempty
        action publishes one atomic store revision. Surviving subscriptions observe
        missing after clear and may reacquire normally.

        Placeholder is descriptor-owned projection metadata, never an implicit write or
        outcome. Unbound reads use committed base; actor-bound reads use base plus only
        that actor's ordered preview layers. Updaters read committed base; equal
        authoritative writes may refresh freshness but only effective-value changes emit
        value changes. Tags derive only from canonical K. Placeholder defaults/status
        cannot create a family; another actor's overlay and implicit canonical writes are
        excluded. Passive reads do not alter ownership, freshness, revision, or work.

        Resource snapshot identity is descriptor/K. P is complete executable input;
        K is the ordered tuple from key(P); passive reads take K and execution takes P.
        Equal keys do not switch a running generation's pinned P. Hydrated key-only
        entries remain passive until a live binding supplies P. Resource projections
        expose the retained family-specific status/generation/failure/retained-value/
        collection shape delegated to @plan.PublicSurface.O3; limits are not state/cache
        entry counts. Actor projection derives from current actor-owned bindings plus
        shared exact descriptor/K StoreState. Releasing a binding removes that actor's
        local projection but does not define canonical deletion; normal collection owns
        eviction. Any actor may passively read an admitted same-runtime canonical entry
        it never materialized. A missing exact read returns synthetic absent/idle without
        inserting a record, acquiring a lease, changing freshness, advancing revision,
        or starting work. No parallel state aliases/cross-product conditional types,
        recursive type inspection, generic refs, unconditional idle retention rule, or
        cross-runtime sharing.

        Reader example from SNAPSHOTS.md snapshot-first forms:
        ```ts
        const snapshot = actor.getSnapshot();
        const orderKey = snapshot.O.orderById.key({ orderId });
        const order = snapshot.O.orderById.getData(orderKey);
        const orderState = snapshot.O.orderById.getState(orderKey);
        const submitState = snapshot.O.submitIntent.getState([submissionId]);
        ```
        Named family readers take canonical K; execution plans retain complete P. No
        generic registry, bound entry, subscription, retry, reset, seed, retention, or
        mutation method is implied. Exact-reader example from the snapshot-reader contract:
        ```ts
        const key = O.orderById.key({ orderId });
        const data = O.orderById.getData(key);
        const state = O.orderById.getState(key);
        ```
        No resources.get, transactions.get, enumeration, ref/byKey/byLane/require,
        subscription/retry/reset/seed/retention/mutation, generic registry, or execution
        method in a selector. Synchronous reads cannot run Effect/runSync, acquire Scope,
        schedule work, or mutate runtime/store state. Unbound reads are committed base;
        transaction updaters read canonical base; actor snapshots add only their own
        ordered overlays. Tags derive only from canonical K.


        Preserve the operation descriptor example from SEMANTICS.md:
        ```ts
        // Named declaration shape; each descriptor is admitted by the compiled machine plan.
        type OperationExamples = {
          orderById: "resource"; // key(P), lookup(P), subscribe(P)
          submitIntent: "transaction"; // key(P), commit(P)
          updates: "stream"; // key(P), subscribe(P)
        };
        ```
    O2  Transaction admission, previews, and fencing
        Transaction identity is exact descriptor plus canonical K; attempts are
        actor-local generations. Completion requires actor ownership, descriptor/K, and
        generation match. Old attempts are bounded inspection evidence, not an actor-
        lifetime registry. No generic transaction registry, cross-actor lane, or
        descriptor-only identity for keyed transactions. An omitted key projector means
        K = []. Each admitted occurrence settles once. Retry is a new explicit event.
        Shared identity is owned by @plan.RuntimeV2.O2 and @plan.RuntimeV2.O1.
        Actor incarnation is owned by @plan.RuntimeV2.O5.

        Concurrency policies are actor-local per exact descriptor/K:

        | Policy | Admission | Completion authority |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | reject | decline second active attempt; no occurrence | current attempt |
        | cancel | cancel prior, then admit replacement | replacement |
        | allow | admit independent generations | current generation only for projection/route |
        | serialize | queue exact-key materialized P FIFO | dequeued occurrence |

        Policy rules:

        - `reject` consumes no ordinal.
        - `cancel` settles the prior attempt and removes its local preview. The replacement
          receives a new ordinal.
        - `allow` retains ordered preview layers.
        - `serialize` previews only at dequeue; canceled queued work starts no adapter.
        - Every admitted policy settles once; stale results are fenced.

        No public transaction scope/lane/cross-actor scheduler,
        automatic retry, partially expanded mixed-target mutation, or terminal-history
        registry.

        External transaction cancellation is truthful across the irreversible boundary.
        Before dispatch, durably record remote identity/boundary state. Before the
        irreversible boundary, abort may classify interruption without a mapped outcome.
        After it, stop local observation and fence late publication; expose unknown or
        reconciliation-required truth without claiming rollback. Reconciliation reuses
        remote idempotency identity; compensation is a separate explicit operation.
        Local preview may be discarded before the boundary. Do not create automatic retry
        as a new request, implicit outbox/saga/rollback API, or remote-effect reversal
        claim.

        Each optimistic overlay carries initiating actor incarnation, transaction
        occurrence, descriptor, and K; only that actor reads it. Success promotes
        atomically to canonical StoreState then fanouts. Failure, defect, or pre-boundary
        interruption removes only that layer and replays remaining layers. Promotion uses
        base-revision CAS; changed base preserves remote canonical truth and creates an
        initiating conflict issue. After boundary, local provisional removal retains
        unknown/reconciliation-required projection; non-initiators receive only canonical
        facts. Overlay is never shared canonical truth. Canonical promotion/rollback is
        fenced and atomic.
    O3  Activities, streams, timers, and cancellation
        Activity identity uses stable compiled declaration slots. A continuing binding
        identity is slot + operation kind + exact descriptor + K; P is retained input,
        and activation generation is monotonic instance metadata. Equal fresh plans retain
        work. Changed selector/kind/descriptor/K releases old once and acquires new.
        false/null release only their own declaration. Finite resource/transaction work
        comes only from accepted event-transition actions. Continuing work comes from
        state activities or one independent onMemory declaration. Each onMemory entry
        returns one plan, false, or null and owns/releases only itself. Resources/streams
        are owned by activities or onMemory; streams are not resource-store entries and
        have no actor cancel. A timer consumes its due generation once. Schedule its next refresh
        only after
        settlement. Failure waits one interval; no implicit retry occurs. onMemory.select
        uses shared selector equality. Activation/reentry gets (current, previous: undefined).
        Later changes get the immediately preceding value.
        Compound activity starts parent-to-child and stops child-to-parent. One-shot timer
        generations, explicit refresh polling, one binding per declaration, and one
        app-owned aggregate resource/stream are allowed. No unsubscribe handles,
        runtime-sized arrays, subscribeMany/subscribeEach, timer-inferred finite actions,
        implicit retry, or unchanged compound-timer reset on descendant transition.

        Activity bindings run under Effect.scoped. FiberMap owns keyed cancel replacement;
        FiberSet owns independent allow work; one keyed Queue worker owns serialize.
        Register cancellation and its fence before any scheduler can execute work. Use no
        manual child Scope. Finalizers run once in reverse acquisition order. StoreKernel
        owns exact-key lookup generations/in-flight leases; actor Scopes
        own registrations/activity leases. Suspension is one serialized lane; resume
        waits for finalizers before reconciling continuing declarations/due timers. Queued
        finite interruption need not start an adapter. Continuing stream close has no
        mapped outcome. Retain absolute timers and a minimal ignored-interruption fact.
        A cleanup defect leaves actor suspended and blocks resume until owner/runtime
        disposal; never replay finite work/emissions/input/initialization or claim
        irreversible remote reversal.

        Planned release is cleanup, not a routed outcome. Release activities and publish
        cleanup facts if needed. Never synthesize a domain outcome or invoke an authored
        interruption mapper. Cleanup defects become issues/receipts without rolling back
        the already published transition. Normal remote completion, interruption, and
        release failure remain distinct and release exactly once. Behaviorally visible
        phased remote work requires an explicitly owned AppPlan actor; a purely
        operational lease may be Effect.acquireRelease in a scoped stream activity. No
        child actor/activity equivalent, new lifetime registry, or hidden cancellation
        channel.

        Every admitted finite occurrence is fenced by actor incarnation, operation kind,
        descriptor ID, canonical K, and a one-based monotonic non-reused ordinal. Shared
        work also uses the exact Store generation/lease epoch. Match every applicable
        fence immediately before completion publication. A later actor incarnation gets
        a new ordinal/generation and cannot inherit the former. Occurrences settle once. Stale facts
        may finalize only bounded evidence of their own
        occurrence. They cannot mutate later incarnations, reused refs, collected entries,
        or current mapped events. Hydration retains bounded cursors and never replays external work;
        restored
        streams create a new generation from executable P. Stream emissions create no
        occurrences. Continuing stream identity is actor-local declaration identity;
        this remains private and creates no public occurrence handle.
    O4  Passive operation snapshots and readers
        Transaction snapshot exposes exact descriptor and K, plus generation only where
        @plan.PublicSurface.O3 requires. Preserve success/failure/defect/interruption and
        post-boundary
        unknown/reconcileRequired lanes; complete Cause remains private issue/TurnRecord
        fact. Exact closed TransactionSnapshot<A,E,K> field presence, generation exposure,
        terminal retention, and collection are delegated to @plan.PublicSurface.O3; absent
        fields remain absent. No raw Cause, generic transaction registry, accumulated old
        attempts in ordinary snapshots, new public union, or public occurrence handle.
        Projection follows current actor binding. Activation, passive reads, completion,
        and reconciliation never admit/readmit finite commit; commits enter only through
        accepted actions. Attempt history belongs in TurnRecords/inspection. Removing,
        replacing, suspending, disposing, or reentering a binding changes only the
        delegated @plan.PublicSurface.O3 projection; do not invent an attempt or second snapshot
        source.

        Continuing resource/stream projection follows named family and K, never a generic
        stream registry. Equal compiled declaration identity retains generation and
        original P; changed slot/kind/descriptor/K releases old once and admits
        replacement. Stream projection retains status, hasValue, latest value when
        present, emission count, declaration generation, and terminal status. Emissions
        are projection-only; durable machine state changes only through mapped events or
        explicit setData. Resource subscriptions observe canonical values. Independent
        streams across actors are allowed; a duplicate live stream declaration by one
        actor for same descriptor/K rejects before replacing/releasing the existing
        declaration. Different actors remain independent. Accepted stream status values
        are idle/running/complete/typed failure/defect/interruption. No generic
        snapshot.streams.get, cross-actor stream dedup, emission replay, key-to-input
        inversion, child surface, or terminal restart. Hydration rematerializes live
        declarations from executable P after pending outcomes drain; missing P fails
        closed and invents no history. Terminal streams do not restart. Missing input
        has a precise diagnostic. Any actor may passively read admitted same-runtime
        resource identity without acquiring, starting, refreshing, mutating, or changing
        collection; a missing read does not materialize StoreState.

        Timer names are machine-wide and unique; snapshot.timers.get(name) accepts only
        the inferred timer-name union. Timer state is exact active leaf. Compound timers
        start on compound activation, retain deadline through descendant changes, cancel
        on compound exit, and restart only on exact reenter. One-shot configuration
        generation and explicit event targeting are allowed; after refresh polling uses
        an existing after timer plus explicit refresh event, never finite action work.
        Due consumes its generation once and publishes fired whether guard accepts or
        rejects; timer history is evidence, not ordinary snapshot history. No timer-owned
        finite actions, fake domain events, deadline reset on unrelated descendant turn,
        or timer identity outside machine.

        Preserve the timer-reader contract's typed reader union:
        ```ts
        type TimerSnapshot<State, Name> =
          | { status: "idle"; name: Name }
          | { status: "scheduled"; name: Name; state: State;
              generation: number; startedAt: number; dueAt: number }
          | { status: "fired"; name: Name; state: State;
              generation: number; startedAt: number; dueAt: number;
              firedAt: number }
          | { status: "interrupt"; name: Name; state: State;
              generation: number; startedAt: number; dueAt: number };
        ```

        The cross-cut proof index covers exact state, ref, event, input, context, memory,
        descriptor, K, selected-value, and timer-name types. Family reads use K; plans
        require P. Removed generic, child, final-node, registered-view, and disposal
        surfaces remain absent.

        Runtime proof is required for these behaviors:

        - Passive missing reads and canonical sharing with independent ownership.
        - Explicit write fencing, production completion, capture, and actor-effective reads.
        - Overlay promotion/rollback, occurrence fencing, and latest-stream projection.
        - Hydration without emission replay and passive operation-read reactivity.

        Type-only or source-text proofs cannot establish runtime behavior.
        Keep operation unions/collection in @plan.PublicSurface.O3 and host writes in
        @plan.Hosts.O2. Do not establish a second source of truth or expose internal owners.
decisions
    D1  One canonical store owner
        StoreKernel owns committed operation state and generations. StoreFanout sends revision 
        facts; actors reread current state and keep local ownership.
    D2  Actor ownership controls work
        Accepted event actions admit finite operations. Actor scopes own continuing bindings; actor 
        incarnation and generation fences reject stale completion.
constraints
    C1  Canonical identity is exact and bounded
        Use descriptor ID plus canonical K within one runtime. Keep executable P separate, and 
        reject unsupported values before mutation or work.
    C2  Passive reads stay passive
        Named readers accept K and never acquire ownership, refresh, mutate, schedule, or start 
        work. Do not add generic registries or implicit operation methods.
questions
    Q1  Pinned Effect scheduling APIs remain to verify
        Which rc.112 APIs implement cancellation registration before work starts and preserve
        the selected keyed concurrency policies? The architecture note names beta.86;
        this repository pins rc.112. Verify the pinned APIs and do not rely on
        `startImmediately` for correctness or ordering. Recommended general Effect
        composition remains nonnormative.
verification
    V1  Store and resource behavior
        Establish @O1 when these scenarios pass:

        - Share one exact descriptor/K across actors without sharing their lifetimes.
        - Isolate canonical state across runtimes and reread after stale revision facts.
        - Order fanout after the initiating actor publication and acknowledgment.
        - Capture only before or after a completed commit/publication boundary.
        - Fail counter overflow before mutation or partial publication.

        Expected: one canonical owner and no stale fanout truth or half-published capture.
        Candidate paths: `actor-operation.test.ts`, `actor-operation-facts.test.ts`,
        `actor-operation-results.test.ts`, `actors.test.ts`, and `copies.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        Explicit StoreKernel, StoreFanout, and DehydrateBarrier proofs remain pending.
    V2  Resource keys, readers, and retention
        Establish @O1 when these scenarios pass:

        - Keep complete executable P separate from canonical K.
        - Reject invalid keys before mutation or work.
        - Share canonical data while preserving independent actor ownership.
        - Keep missing reads passive and exact subscriptions stable across equivalent plans.
        - Fence explicit writes and validate invalidation/clear expansion atomically.
        - Release actor bindings without implying canonical deletion.

        Expected: readers and projections cause no passive side effects. Candidate paths:
        `actor-operation.test.ts`, `actor-operation-facts.test.ts`,
        `actor-operation-results.test.ts`, `structural-copy-owner.test.ts`, and
        `copies.test.ts` under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        Store, invalidation-batch, subscription, and collection coverage remains pending.
    V3  Activity and lifecycle ownership
        Establish @O2 and @O3 when these scenarios pass:

        - Exercise reject, cancel, allow, and serialize for exact descriptor/K.
        - Prove reject consumes no ordinal and canceled queued work starts no adapter.
        - Preserve ordered previews and fence replacement, incarnation, and store generation.
        - Distinguish cancellation before and after an irreversible remote boundary.
        - Keep cleanup release unmapped and run finalizers once in reverse order.
        - Retain equal declarations; release changed declarations exactly once.
        - Restore continuing work without replaying finite work or stream emissions.

        Expected: one current settlement, honest remote uncertainty, and no stale write.
        Candidate tests live under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`:

        - `actor-activities-owner.test.ts`, `actor-operation.test.ts`, and
          `actor-operation-facts.test.ts`.
        - `actor-operation-results.test.ts`, `actor-operation-fixture.ts`, and
          `actors.test.ts`.
        Store, transaction, and stream policy cases remain pending unless explicit in tests.
    V4  Snapshot and reader behavior
        Establish @O4 when these scenarios pass:

        - Read absent keys without creating StoreState or starting work.
        - Separate canonical resource values from actor-effective overlays.
        - Keep transaction projections actor-local and streams latest-value only.
        - Reject duplicate live stream declarations for one actor and descriptor/K.
        - Type timer names and expose only the exact timer status union.
        - Preserve compound deadlines and consume each due generation once.

        Expected: projections cannot create, retry, restart, or invent operation history.
        Candidate paths: `actor-operation.test.ts`, `actors.test.ts`,
        `actor-operation-results.test.ts`, and `actor-activities-owner.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        A typed snapshot fixture and full runtime proof matrix remain pending.
